  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nAn upper-level trough over eastern Canada will deepen and  \ncarve south into New England tonight into Monday. Guidance \ncontinues to favor a slightly slower eastward progression, with\nthe trough potentially closing off. Deep southerly to \nsouthwesterly flow will advect a plume of moisture rich air into\nthe region, driving PWATs values around 2.0 inches, within an \nenvironment with deep warm-cloud depths (>13kft). The overnight\ntiming will limit overall intensity, and should start Monday \noff with mostly cloudy conditions. The cloud cover will be a \ndefining driver on intensity of any storms throughout Monday as \nupper levels become more favorable with ascent increasing \nthroughout the day. Increasing large-scale ascent on Monday \nwill interact with modest instability (CAPE 500-1000j/kg) will \nproduce multiple rounds of heavy showers and thunderstorms. As \nthings currently look, we can expect tall, skinny CAPE profiles,\nwith a high moisture content, and efficient warm rain \ncollision-coalescence processes. Translation, when it rains it \nwill pour. The storms should be progressive, which should keep \nmost storm total accumulations around 0.5-1. However, the storm\nmovements will be running parallel to the frontal boundary, \nincreasing the potential for storm training and backbuilding \nwith FROPA. Dry antecedent conditions with ample runoff storage \nreduces the widespread flood risk, however localized areas could\nquickly accumulate 3+, so we cant rule out an isolated risk \nof flash flooding. Higher terrain may experience localized \nupslope ascent, further elevating rainfall efficiency and flash \nflood threats. WPC has the region in a level 1 of 4, a marginal \nrisk for localized flash flooding. At this time it is hard to \npin point exactly where the highest risk for flooding would \noccur, and given dry antecedent conditions, have held off on a \nflood watch.\n\nIn addition to the excessive rainfall threat, SPC maintains a \nMarginal Risk (Level 1/5) for severe weather for Monday\nafternoon; the primary hazard will be localized damaging wind \ngusts from water- loaded downbursts. CAMs continue to suggest \nthat storms will develop along the cold front moving west to \neast across the area, strengthening in New Hampshire towards \npeak heating. As mentioned above, we will see some modest\ninstability with MLCAPE in the 500 to 1000 J/kg range.\nThe instability along the front will be collocated with 40 to \n45 knots of effective layer shear. Thus, the more established \nupdrafts will have the potential to produce some isolated \ndamaging wind gusts. As is often the case around here, much of \nthe severe potential will depend on how much New Hampshire can \nclear out after the morning convection moves through. If we get \nsome more breaks, we could see pockets of MLCAPE closer to that \n1000 J/kg value but if we remain socked in, instability will be \nmore limited.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nTuesday through Friday...\nThe frontal boundary will weaken south of the region on Tuesday,\nproviding fair conditions and a brief reduction in the \nheat/humidity. By Wednesday through the weekend, the broad \nupper level trough shifts back over the Great Lakes, and with \nthe Bermuda High offshore establishing deep southwesterly flow \nacross the area. This will support warm and humid conditions,\nwith shower and thunderstorm chances (2535%) diurnally driven.\nSome guidance is showing embedded shortwave energy Thursday \nthat could enhance coverage, so have highest pops at that point \nin the extended period. While exact timing and intensity remain \nuncertain this far out, diurnal heating will favor the highest \nstorm coverage during the afternoon and evening hours.",
